A||N||4||bHooper||Slave in the house!||||10||
Z||000000||bHooper||05-18-2000||01:35 PM||||I got my new GM Slave Cylinder today, and have a question.<P>As it is fully extended, do I need to compress it prior to intalling it against the  release arm?<P>I have also purchase DOT 5 brake fluid for the clutch line.  I intend to purge the line with brake cleaner, and use this instead of DOT 3, as I do not have a heat shield for the slave (at the moment) and this is less likely to boil.<P>hoop||||reg||10
Z||000001||Fiasco||05-18-2000||03:19 PM||||I think you have to...<BR>If I remember right, when I did mine, I put the cylinder on the bracket without the pushrod in it. (the rod was not permanently inserted in the cyl.) -I just kinda finger-tightened it on so as to leave play. Then I cheated it to one side a bit and shoved the rod in along side the clutch fork arm. -It's not so hard to push in without hydraulic lines connected-  And then tightened the mounting bolts. (make sure it is inline with the fork arm if there is any lateral play) Then connect the line, fill, and bleed. -and bleed... and bleed...<BR>As for the switch to DOT 5, I don't know that I'd use the brake cleaner. If you flush enough DOT5 through, you'll have most of the DOT3 out. It's hard to get every trace of something out without tearing the whole system out and rebuilding the master cyl.- and I don't think you want brake cleaner staying in there.<BR>By the way, I use plain old DOT 3 and have no problems, and I don't have a heat shield, either. (am I supposed to?) -But, whatever works. <BR>-Hope this makes sense.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||05-18-2000||04:13 PM||||Fiasco, what you said, makes some sense.  of course, you never know until you start working on it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>as for changing the hydraulic fluids... according to the import repair place that I purchased the DOT 5 from; flush with brake cleaner, then blow out the line with compressed air.  it makes sense to me, and they have good experience from working on any  and every kind of odd european import that you could imagine.<P>i will let yoiu know later tonight... after i go for a drive in my drive way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodney||05-18-2000||05:07 PM||||No need to clean the system. Just flush some thru. The bottle I bought said to do the same thing. The residual DOT 3 is not a problem it seems.<P>I have the DOT 5 in my clutch system. So smooth and no rust worries!!!!!!!<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||05-19-2000||07:15 AM||||Works like a champ!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Went for a drive around the hood tonight!!<P>Wow!!!! <B>What have I been missing?!</B><P>I did clean out the lines, ran a whole bottle of DOT 5 through them, afterwards... BEAUTIFULL!<P>Now I gotta get the body on her, and get her inspected!<P>BTW, Rodney, guess what I found in my bolt box today?.... thats right, I found my original lower shift cable bracket... now I gots two!  Oh well.<P>Smiling and soon to be driving in Greensboro,<P>hoop!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
